  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1201 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA couple weak shortwaves will move through the region Sunday into \nMonday bringing light snow to the mountains with little to no snow \nexpected for the lower elevations of Central Montana. Temperatures \nwill remain on the cooler side on Sunday before starting a gradual \nwarmup on Monday that will continue through the rest of the week. \nUpper-level ridging briefly builds in on Tuesday/Wednesday allowing \nfor drier conditions to return before the ridge gets flattened out \nby Thursday morning. A weak shortwave is expected to move through \nduring Thanksgiving day bringing a chance for rain/snow across the \nregion. Ensemble guidance shows upper-level ridging trying to build \nback into the western CONUS Friday into Saturday, however, there is \nsome remaining uncertainty on how strong the ridge will be and where \nthe ridge axis will be located which leaves some uncertainty on how \nthe finer details will play out through the weekend.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFog Tonight and Tomorrow Night:\n\nPatchy dense fog will remain a concern through the remainder of the \nnight with frost deposition being an additional concern for \nlocations where fog is able to persist. This may lead to slippery \nroads and sidewalks through the mid-morning hours. Anyone heading \nout should be prepared for sudden reductions in visibility and road \nconditions.\n\nWhile some patchy fog has already been observed, fog has held off in \nmany locations as a low stratus deck persists. While fog has \nstruggled to develop in these areas, there are still two possible \nscenarios where fog may develop at some point during the night. The \nfirst possibility is if the clouds temporarily break up and the \nsecond is the stratus deck continues to lower until it eventually \nconnects with the ground. Right now there is a 20-40% chance for \nadditional fog development across the lower elevations of Central \nand Southwest Montana through the late morning hours.\n\nSunday night into Monday night will be similar in that the \nenvironment will be close to saturation. But the winds might be just \nstrong enough to prevent fog development. Nonetheless there is still \na 20% chance for fog development along the river valleys and across \nportions of North-Central Montana with fog coverage potentially \nbeing even more sporadic that what is observed tonight.\n\nRegarding tonight, because the fog has been quite patchy and \nvisibilities have generally stayed above 3 miles, a Dense Fog \nAdvisory will not be issued at this time. But this will be an ever-\nevolving situation and should more widespread dense fog start to \ndevelop then an advisory may be considered at a later time.\n\n\nSnow on Sunday/Monday:\n\nLight snow will settle across the Continental Divide and the \nmountains of Southwest Montana over the morning hours and continue \nthrough the rest of the day. A few flurries may make it out onto the \nNorthern Plains, however, confidence is low and minimal snow \naccumulation is expected. By Monday, the chance for snow will be \nmore confined to Southwest Montana, particularly along the \nMontana/Idaho border. New snowfall accumulations will be minimal but \nthe associated low cloud decks may lead to reduce visibility along \narea passes at times.  -thor",
